  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nCurrently, fair weather cumulus is over much of the CWA per daytime \nheating along with mid to high clouds moving from the northwest and \nover central SD per downstream side of the ridge. A hazy sky \ncontinues due to ongoing smoke aloft with HRRR smoke model \ncontinuing to indicate periods of elevated smoke through at least \nThursday. This has been incorporated in the sky grids. \n\nThe Northern Plains remains in northwest flow with a ridge over the \nwestern CONUS and trough over the eastern CONUS. Not much movement \nexpected with this pattern aloft for the next several days. At the \nsurface, the CWA remains on the southern/southeastern side of a high \npressure system, with the center over ND/MT/Saskatchewan as of 18Z \nkeeping the area dry. This high will broaden over the Northern \nPlains as it drifts southeast through tonight with the center of the \nhigh over the eastern Dakotas/MN by 12Z Wednesday with the CWA on \nthe western side of it by Wednesday evening with overall dry \nconditions continuing. However, with a weak wave rounding the top of \nthe ridge then downstream in this northwest flow aloft, CAMs agree \noverall on a band or bands of elevated showers (and maybe a rumble \nof thunder) tracking across central SD, with the bulk of the QPF \nover south central SD late tonight through Wednesday morning. \nProbability of QPF>0.10\" is 40-70% along and south of a line from \nMission Ridge to Chamberlain with the highest percent over Jones and \nLyman Counties. Prob of at least 0.25\" at 20-40% for this same \nlocation. Additional elevated showers and weak thunderstorms (pops \n20-40) are possible over south central SD once again Thursday \nthrough Thursday evening per another weak wave aloft rounding the \nridge. Probability of 0.10\" is less this time around at 20-40% over \nsouth central SD. Slight pops (20% or less) are possible late \nSaturday into early Sunday with a passing frontal boundary/weak low, \nhowever, the ridge and heat may influence this potential so low \nconfidence as of now.\n\nThe good news with this high is that temperatures will be a bit \ncooler giving us a break from the heat and humidity with highs for \nWednesday only in the upper 70s to the mid 80s, warmest over north \ncentral SD. Highs for Thursday will increase by a few degrees across \nthe CWA, ranging in the lower to upper 80s. However, the ridge out \nwest will shift eastward Friday and similar to last week will park \nitself right over the central CONUS over the weekend through at \nleast the middle of next week as the top of the ridge will amplify \nwell into Canada with its mid level high extending from central \nRockies through the Central Plains. Highs are forecast to reach the \n90s west of the James River on Friday and upper 90s to lower 100s \nacross the CWA this weekend with the hotter day being Saturday where \nsome portions of north central SD could reach up to 106! Dewpoints \nlook to be similar to last week overall ranging in the upper 50s to \nthe upper 60s, highest James Valley and eastward. Closer to time, we \nwill see how the humidity/peak evapotranspiration will affect high \ntemps for this area as more humidity will equal overall highs being \n\"cooler\". Drier air over central SD should allow for temps to reach \nthe triple digits easily. Once again, GFS is running a few degrees \nhotter than the EC at this time for surface temps-850mb both \nSaturday and Sunday. Example, 850mb temps peak heating Saturday \nafternoon is 34C GFS vs 29C EC at KABR. Looking at NBM 5.0 vs 5.2, \nthere is only about a degree difference in high temps over central \nSD this weekend (giving higher confidence on achieving these highs) \nwhereas there is a 1-3 degrees spread James Valley and eastward with \nNBM 5.2 being lower trying to correct this bias, therefore giving \nlower confidence in exact highs. So closer to time, we may need to \nlower the temps James Valley and eastward like we did last week as \nall the models were running several degrees higher than what \nactually occurred. Currently, the 25-75th spread per NBM is 5-7 \ndegrees for the weekend. HeatRisk these days will range from \nModerate to Major meaning this heat will affect those sensitive to \nheat or without cooling/hydration. Closer to time, a Heat Headline \nwill likely be needed as heat indices look to be in the triple \ndigits (with the exception of the higher elevations of the Coteau) \nSaturday and central SD Sunday. Temps will still remain hot over the \nCWA with highs ranging in the 90s early next week.",
